<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Bulge</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1944">1944</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ardennes Offensive · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the Bulge was the last major German offensive on the Western Front in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, launched on 16 December 1944 through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>. German armies drove a bulge into the American lines but failed to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, were held at Bastogne and pushed back by late January 1945. Northern Luxembourg suffered heavy destruction.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-bulge.jpg" alt="Battle of the Bulge" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Army · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:117th_Infantry_North_Carolina_NG_at_St._Vith_1945.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the breakout from Normandy at the end of July 1944, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> advanced toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> faster than expected, leaving troops tired and supply lines stretched thin. They captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> intact in September, but it was not operational until 28 November. Adolf Hitler believed that a blow in the west could split the Allies and force the Americans and British to a separate peace, giving Germany time to produce new weapons and concentrate in the east. After the war this was generally judged unrealistic given Allied air superiority.</p><p>The offensive aimed to stop Allied use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> and to split their lines. The Germans called it Unternehmen Wacht am Rhein, a name implying a defensive posture. The name Battle of the Bulge came from the press, from the way the Allied line bulged inward on wartime news maps.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>At 5:30 on 16 December 1944 the Germans opened with a 90-minute barrage along a front of 130 kilometres. They achieved total surprise, through Allied overconfidence, poor air reconnaissance in bad weather and faulty intelligence. The region was a rest area held thinly by tired troops and inexperienced replacements. In the north the 2nd and 99th Infantry Divisions held Elsenborn Ridge and forced the German spearhead onto less favourable routes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waffen-ss">Waffen-SS</a> troops under Joachim Peiper shot American prisoners near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malmedy">Malmedy</a>.</p><p>In the centre the Germans encircled two regiments of the 106th Division at the Schnee Eifel, the most serious American reverse of the campaign. Bastogne was surrounded, and the farthest German advance, to Foy-Notre-Dame, was stopped by the 2nd Armored Division on 24 December. The weather improved from 23 December, letting Allied aircraft strike, and on 26 December the lead element of George S. Patton's Third Army reached Bastogne from the south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The offensive was effectively broken by 27 December, but fighting went on for another month as Patton attacked north and Bernard Montgomery south to reduce the bulge. On 1 January 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> attacked Allied airfields in Operation Bodenplatte, destroying about 465 aircraft but losing 277, and was left ineffective for the rest of the war. It was the largest and bloodiest single battle fought by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the war. German casualties are put at 81,000 to 103,900 by Allied estimates. The battle destroyed much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s armoured strength and set back the Allied advance into Germany by several weeks.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Bulge” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>American troops liberated most of the country in September 1944, but the north suffered heavily in the <b>Battle of the Bulge</b> that winter.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Second World War the Wehrmacht's daily radio report opened with its tune until 1941, and its title was the code name of the German offensive of 1944 now known as the <b>Battle of the Bulge</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-wacht-am-rhein">Die Wacht am Rhein</a></li></ul></section>
